		<profileDesc>
			<abstract>
				<p>Annotator disagreement poses a significant challenge in subjective tasks like hate speech detection. In this paper, we introduce a novel variant of the HateWiC task that explicitly models annotator agreement by estimating the proportion of annotators who classify the meaning of a term as hateful. To tackle this challenge, we explore the use of Llama 3 models fine-tuned through Direct Preference Optimization (DPO). Our experiments show that while LLMs perform well for majority-based hate classification, they struggle with the more complex agreement-aware task. DPO fine-tuning offers improvements, particularly when applied to instruction-tuned models. Our results emphasize the need for improved modeling of subjectivity in hate classification and this study can serve as foundation for future advancements.</p>
			</abstract>
